<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Church of the Holy Apostles, Kars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">930s–940s</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Kümbet Mosque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Church of the Holy Apostles is a 10th-century Armenian church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, built by King Abas I of Bagratid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> between about 930 and 943. A domed tetraconch with reliefs of the twelve apostles on its drum, it served as the cathedral of the Bagratid capital. Converted into a mosque under Ottoman rule, it was a Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> church from 1878 to 1918 and is now a mosque again.</p></div>
